\documentclass[11pt]{article}
\usepackage[margin=1in]{geometry}
\title{Runoff Retention on Three\\Extensive Green Roofs}
\author{S.~Beaumont and K.~Iversen}
\date{March 2026}
\begin{document}
\maketitle
\tableofcontents
\section{Introduction}
Green roofs are increasingly credited with stormwater benefits, but
retention estimates vary widely across climates and substrates.
We instrumented three extensive roofs on the same city block and
logged outflow through one wet season.
\section{Site and Sensors}
Each roof drains through a single downpipe fitted with a
tipping-bucket gauge and a data logger.
Table \ref{tab:calibration} reports the laboratory calibration of
the tipping-bucket gauges, and Table \ref{tab:calibration} repeats
the procedure after field installation.
Figure \ref{fig:drfit} shows the sensor drift observed over the
season.
\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\begin{tabular}{|l|c|c|}
\hline
Gauge & Slope (ml/tip) & R$^2$ \\
\hline
A & 4.81 & 0.998 \\
\hline
B & 4.77 & 0.997 \\
\hline
C & 4.83 & 0.998 \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\caption{Laboratory calibration of the tipping-bucket rain gauges.}
\label{tab:calibration}
\end{table}
\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\begin{tabular}{|l|c|c|}
\hline
Gauge & Slope (ml/tip) & R$^2$ \\
\hline
A & 4.92 & 0.995 \\
\hline
B & 4.88 & 0.996 \\
\hline
C & 4.95 & 0.995 \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\caption{Field calibration repeated after installation on the roofs.}
\label{tab:calibration}
\end{table}
\begin{figure}[h]
\centering
\fbox{\parbox[c][4cm][c]{0.6\linewidth}{\centering Drift curve placeholder}}
\caption{Sensor drift over the season for the three outflow gauges.}
\label{fig:drift}
\end{figure}
\section{Results}
Retention averaged 62 percent across the season, with the deepest
substrate retaining 71 percent.
Event-scale retention fell as antecedent moisture rose, and the
three roofs converged during the wettest month.
\section{Discussion}
The convergence during wet months suggests substrate depth matters
most at the start of the wet season.
Drift corrections changed seasonal totals by less than two percent,
so we report uncorrected values throughout.
\section{Conclusion}
Extensive green roofs on this block retained roughly three fifths of
seasonal rainfall, and simple tipping-bucket gauges proved adequate
for event-scale monitoring.
\end{document}

